---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead
      title="Beige Box Privacy Policy"
      description="Privacy Policy for Beige Box, an iOS app of original pretend vintage computers with real web browsing and files saved on your device."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <main id="main">
      <article class="policy tile">
        <p class="tile-kicker">Beige Box</p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="date"><strong>Effective:</strong> November 2, 2026</p>

        <p>
          Beige Box is an iOS app with five original pretend computers from earlier eras. You can
          write, draw, program in BASIC, play games and browse the real web on them. There is no
          account to create, no login, and no server run by this app. This policy explains what stays
          on your device, what happens when you browse the web, and what happens when you buy or
          restore the one-time unlock.
        </p>

        <h2>What stays on your device</h2>
        <p>
          Documents you write in Write, programs you save from the BASIC prompt, and pictures you
          save from Paint are stored as ordinary files in the app's Documents folder on your device.
          All five computers share this one disk. Because they are ordinary files, they also appear
          in the Files app under On My iPhone (or On My iPad) › Beige Box, and in Finder file sharing
          when you connect your device to a computer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app also stores a few settings on your device: which computer you picked, whether you
          finished the introduction, whether sound is muted, your screen colour and case colour
          choices, your best Worm and Breakwater scores, your Pairs best move count and wins, and a
          randomly generated guest name for the bulletin board. Game records stay on your device.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app does not upload your files or settings. If you use iCloud Backup or back up your
          device to a computer, Apple's backup may include them under your backup settings.
        </p>

        <h2>Browsing the web</h2>
        <p>
          On the four desktop computers, Surf displays live, interactive websites directly from
          your device using Apple's WebKit. STUDIO 86 displays those pages in grayscale. HOMEROOM 82
          fetches pages directly from your device and shows them as text. Browsing does not pass
          through any server run by this app, and the app does not send your browsing history to the
          developer.
        </p>
        <p>
          As with any web browser, the websites you visit receive your IP address, the pages you
          request, and a browser identifier that names the app. They may set cookies or store other
          site data. The four desktop computers share WebKit's website storage within the app on
          your device. Anything you type into a web page is sent to that website.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you type words that are not a web address, the app sends them as a search to
          DuckDuckGo. Websites and DuckDuckGo handle the information they receive under their own
          policies, including
          <a href="https://duckduckgo.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >DuckDuckGo's Privacy Policy</a
          >.
        </p>

        <h2>The BeigeNet bulletin board</h2>
        <p>
          BeigeNet is currently a local demonstration on your device, not a shared message board. The
          welcome posts are sample messages that ship with the app. Messages you post there stay in
          memory on your device. They are not sent anywhere, other people cannot see them, and they
          are cleared when the app is fully closed. Your guest name is generated on your device and
          is not sent anywhere.
        </p>

        <h2>Purchases</h2>
        <p>
          Beige Box is free to download. Using the computers requires a single one-time purchase.
          Apple processes the purchase itself; this app never receives your payment card details.
          Apple's signed purchase record on your device decides whether the unlock is active, so the
          computers keep working offline.
        </p>
        <p>
          The app uses RevenueCat as its purchase layer. RevenueCat processes purchase history and an
          anonymous identifier that it assigns to your installation, so the app can complete and
          restore purchases and give the developer purchase analytics. The app turns off
          RevenueCat's automatic device identifier collection and diagnostics. It does not send
          RevenueCat a name, email address, custom user ID, advertising identifier, your files, or
          your browsing. Apple and RevenueCat handle the information they receive under their own
          policies:
          <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Apple's Privacy Policy</a
          >
          and
          <a href="https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >RevenueCat's Privacy Policy</a
          >.
        </p>

        <h2>Exporting files</h2>
        <p>
          You can choose Export in My Disk to send a file through the iOS share sheet. That is always
          something you start, and you choose where the file goes. Once you send or save it somewhere,
          the app or service you chose handles it under its own terms.
        </p>

        <h2>What the app does not do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>No advertising, ad networks, or advertising identifier.</li>
          <li>No app-owned account, login, or cloud sync.</li>
          <li>No tracking across other apps and websites, and no App Tracking Transparency prompt.</li>
          <li>No general app-usage analytics or crash reporting. RevenueCat's purchase analytics are described above.</li>
          <li>
            The built-in programs do not request your contacts, location, photos, camera, microphone,
            or notifications. Websites may offer their own file-selection controls. The app detects
            a connected hardware keyboard only to accept typing from it.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Keeping and deleting your information</h2>
        <p>
          You can delete a file with Delete in My Disk or in the Files app. Deleting a file in My Disk
          removes it from your device immediately. Deleting the app removes its files, settings and
          stored website data from that device, but not copies you exported or copies in your device
          backups. Restoring a purchase restores access to the computers; it does not bring back
          deleted files.
        </p>
        <p>
          Purchase records held by Apple and RevenueCat are kept under their own retention practices
          and are not deleted by removing the app. If you have a question about purchase information
          associated with your installation, email the address below. Because the app assigns no
          identity to you, some requests may need information from your Apple purchase receipt.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Surf opens the real web, and the app does not filter website content. The app is not
          designed for children to browse unsupervised; parents can use the device's own parental
          controls. The app does not knowingly collect personal information from anyone, including
          children.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If the app adds a feature that changes what is processed, for example a shared bulletin
          board, a server run by this app, an account, or app-usage analytics, this policy will be updated
          before that feature ships, and the effective date above will change.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about this policy can be sent to
          <a href="mailto:randyventures06@gmail.com">randyventures06@gmail.com</a>.
        </p>
      </article>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .policy {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .policy h2 {
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .date {
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
